WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:02.820
-: So the second use for git restore is

00:02.820 --> 00:05.880
to unstage files that we currently stage.

00:05.880 --> 00:08.160
So if we made a mistake, we stage something

00:08.160 --> 00:10.260
it's going to be included in the next commit

00:10.260 --> 00:12.960
and then we decide, ah, we really don't want that.

00:12.960 --> 00:15.570
We can un stage it using git restore

00:15.570 --> 00:19.260
and then dash dash staged in a file name.

00:19.260 --> 00:22.410
So I'll demonstrate this, let's restore back to head.

00:22.410 --> 00:23.970
So we'll just do get restore.

00:23.970 --> 00:28.970
Whoops, get restore our files, cat and dog.

00:29.850 --> 00:33.420
We'll go back to first, second, and third in both of them.

00:33.420 --> 00:35.430
So now I'm gonna make a new commit in here.

00:35.430 --> 00:37.560
Fourth commit.

00:37.560 --> 00:39.450
So I'll just put that in both of them.

00:39.450 --> 00:41.610
And for the sake of this example

00:41.610 --> 00:45.510
I'm gonna make a file, I'll call it secrets dot txt.

00:45.510 --> 00:48.390
And in here I'll put something like, I don't know

00:48.390 --> 00:52.140
A P i key equals some a P i key

00:52.140 --> 00:57.140
and then credit card number equals and then some fake.

00:57.330 --> 00:59.220
I don't even, I don't think that's the right number

01:00.405 --> 01:01.238
of digits, but let's say that's your credit card number.

01:02.093 --> 01:04.314
Okay, then I forget about this file

01:04.314 --> 01:06.147
and I'm ready to make my next commit.

01:06.147 --> 01:08.670
So I head over here and I type git add dot

01:08.670 --> 01:12.209
and then I have a horrible sinking feeling.

01:12.209 --> 01:13.868
I type get status.

01:13.868 --> 01:16.620
I realized, shoot, I did not want to stage my secrets file.

01:16.620 --> 01:18.000
I don't wanna include that.

01:18.000 --> 01:19.890
Now ideally I would have a get ignore

01:19.890 --> 01:21.460
and it would include that file

01:22.368 --> 01:23.730
but still there are times if you've done a lot

01:23.730 --> 01:26.070
of work and you need to split your changes

01:26.070 --> 01:29.010
up into a couple of commits, you might add a file

01:29.010 --> 01:31.200
that you don't wanna stage, you might wanna remove it.

01:31.200 --> 01:32.370
That's what we're doing here.

01:32.370 --> 01:35.371
I want these two to be included, right?

01:35.371 --> 01:36.698
I want fourth commit.

01:36.698 --> 01:38.034
Fourth commit.

01:38.034 --> 01:40.470
I want that to be included, but I want to unstage this.

01:40.470 --> 01:43.140
So to do that, git restore dash

01:43.140 --> 01:45.900
dash staged and then the file name.

01:45.900 --> 01:47.310
So here we go.

01:47.310 --> 01:52.170
Git restore, dash, dash staged secrets, not stages.

01:52.170 --> 01:53.003
Staged.

01:54.747 --> 01:57.270
Okay, that gets status

01:57.270 --> 02:00.330
and now it's not being tracked, right?

02:00.330 --> 02:02.250
Git doesn't really care about this file anymore.

02:02.250 --> 02:04.920
It's at least not staged to be included in the commit

02:04.920 --> 02:07.050
but the file has not been changed.

02:07.050 --> 02:10.020
So we didn't like delete it or reset anything in it.

02:10.020 --> 02:12.000
It's just not staged for the next commit.

02:12.000 --> 02:17.000
So now I can make my commit fourth commit get status.

02:17.460 --> 02:20.850
Still it was not included and it's still there, you know

02:20.850 --> 02:24.243
just waiting for us to stage it or ideally to ignore it.

02:25.110 --> 02:27.090
So that's kind of confusing, right?

02:27.090 --> 02:32.090
The two purposes for git restore one is to unmodify a file

02:32.460 --> 02:37.200
or files and the other is to un stage file or files.

02:37.200 --> 02:41.190
Fortunately, if you use git status, Git will tell you

02:41.190 --> 02:45.150
at any point it reminds you how to do both of them.

02:45.150 --> 02:48.690
So for example, if I stage a change

02:48.690 --> 02:50.490
which right now I don't have anything

02:50.490 --> 02:55.490
but if I do stage that change, get add secrets dot txt

02:55.740 --> 02:59.010
and I type get status, it tells me right here

02:59.010 --> 03:00.330
this is going to be committed.

03:00.330 --> 03:04.281
If you don't want that use git restore dash

03:04.281 --> 03:07.410
dash staged and then a file name to unstage.

03:07.410 --> 03:09.540
'cause honestly I struggled to remember this

03:09.540 --> 03:12.129
I don't have to do it all that often.

03:12.129 --> 03:14.295
Thankfully git status tells us.

03:14.295 --> 03:15.773
So I mean we could just copy that.

03:17.940 --> 03:22.830
Great. And then the same thing, if I change one of my files

03:22.830 --> 03:25.560
I put some stuff in there, I delete it, great.

03:25.560 --> 03:28.983
Git status, it says hey, you modified that file.

03:30.004 --> 03:32.457
And it's also mentioned to discard the changes

03:32.457 --> 03:33.330
in the working directory.

03:33.330 --> 03:35.400
Use git restore and then the file name.

03:35.400 --> 03:38.457
So I'll do that, get restore cat dot txt

03:38.457 --> 03:40.620
and we're back to where we were.

03:40.620 --> 03:42.630
So you don't have to remember it at all times.

03:42.630 --> 03:45.840
You can just use git status it will remind you what to use.

03:45.840 --> 03:48.548
It does not show you in git status though

03:48.548 --> 03:50.144
that you have this option, right?

03:50.144 --> 03:50.977
To specify a different source.

03:50.977 --> 03:51.843
Not as common.

03:53.076 --> 03:54.853
But this does allow you to reset the contents

03:54.853 --> 03:57.540
of a file to what it looked like 10 commits ago if needed.

03:57.540 --> 03:59.370
So you can use that dash, dash source

03:59.370 --> 04:01.860
it defaults to head as we've seen.

04:01.860 --> 04:03.260
Okay, so that's git restore.
